# Editor / Dev-Only Systems

Components that only exist in the editor or development builds — dev overlays, debug HUDs, event logs, profiler widgets, in-scene test helpers — are typically guarded by `#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD`. Such a type **does not exist in a release player**, so a release build must never *load* a prefab or scene that still references it.

## Why a serialized reference breaks the release build

When a release build loads a scene/prefab that references a stripped-out type, Unity cannot resolve the script and logs:

```
The referenced script (Your.Namespace.DevOverlay) on this Behaviour is missing!
A scripted object (probably Your.Namespace.DevOverlay?) has a different serialization
layout when loading. (Read 32 bytes but expected 48 bytes)
```

These are shipped-to-players errors that never appear in the editor (where the type compiles), so they slip through until someone opens the release console.

That is the constraint. There are two ways to satisfy it — pick by whether the tool has UI.

## A. Prefab-authored, stripped at build time (preferred for UI-bearing tools)

Author the tool on a prefab like any other content, and delete it from the scene during non-development builds with `IProcessSceneWithReport`. This runs on the build's temporary copy of the scene, so committed scenes keep their instance and only the shipped player loses it.

Put a **marker component on the prefab root** and strip on that. The marker is the whole mechanism — an empty `MonoBehaviour` under the same compile guard as the tools it marks:

```csharp
#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD
public sealed class DevToolsRoot : MonoBehaviour
{
}
#endif
```

```csharp
public sealed class DevToolsSceneStripper : IProcessSceneWithReport
{
    public int callbackOrder => 0;

    public void OnProcessScene(Scene scene, BuildReport report)
    {
        // Null report means play mode is starting, not a build — the tools must survive.
        if (report == null || report.summary.options.HasFlag(BuildOptions.Development))
        {
            return;
        }

        GameObject[] roots = scene.GetRootGameObjects();
        for (int i = 0; i < roots.Length; i++)
        {
            if (roots[i].GetComponentInChildren<DevToolsRoot>(true) != null)
            {
                Object.DestroyImmediate(roots[i]);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

- The `report == null` early-out is load-bearing: Unity also invokes scene processors when entering play mode, and stripping there would delete the tools you just dropped in.
- **Match on a component type, never on GameObject name** — a renamed instance must still be caught.
- **Prefer one marker over a list of every dev type.** Listing types works right up until someone adds a dev system and forgets the stripper — and that failure is invisible in the editor, surfacing only as missing scripts in a shipped player's console. A marker makes "on the dev prefab" and "stripped" the same fact.
- **Every component sharing a dev prefab needs the same `#if` guard**, marker included. A component guarded by only `UNITY_EDITOR` sitting on a prefab next to `UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD` siblings becomes a missing script in *development* player builds. Widen the narrow one and let it no-op outside the editor.
- Adding a dev system to an existing dev prefab then needs no stripper edit at all — just put it under the marked root.

This is what makes the tool draggable: it can be committed into a scene without ever reaching a player, and its UI is authored in the Inspector like everything else (see the `no-code-built-ui` rule) instead of being built in C#.

## B. Self-spawn under the guard (for small, no-UI, always-on helpers)

When the tool has no authorable content — a frame-rate clamp, a log forwarder, a static-state hook — a prefab buys nothing. Guard the whole file and let it create itself.

```csharp
#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class DevLogForwarder : MonoBehaviour
{
    // Self-spawns instead of being placed on a prefab/scene: this type is compiled out of
    // release builds, so a serialized reference to it would become a "missing script" +
    // serialization-layout error in the player. Idempotent — bails if one already exists.
    [RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod(RuntimeInitializeLoadType.AfterSceneLoad)]
    private static void EnsureInstance()
    {
        if (FindAnyObjectByType<DevLogForwarder>(FindObjectsInactive.Include) != null)
        {
            return;
        }

        new GameObject(nameof(DevLogForwarder)).AddComponent<DevLogForwarder>();
    }
}
#endif
```

- Make the bootstrap **idempotent** — `AfterSceneLoad` fires on every scene load.
- Provide code defaults; a self-spawned instance has no inspector data.
- Wire providers/config behind the `#if`, not via inspector fields on a shipped asset.

## Choosing

| Situation | Pattern |
|---|---|
| Tool has UI, or should be optional per scene, or wants Inspector tuning | **A** — prefab + stripper |
| Tool is a few lines, no UI, and should always be on in dev | **B** — self-spawn |
| Several dev tools in one project | **A** — one shared dev prefab, one stripper |

Self-spawn is the cheaper answer for one small helper. Once a tool grows UI, or you find yourself wanting it in some scenes but not others, it has outgrown B — the self-spawn hook forces it into every scene and its UI can only be built in code.

## Do not

- Ship a dev-guarded `MonoBehaviour` on a prefab/scene **without** a build-time stripper covering it.
- Let a dev prefab mix compile guards (see A) — the narrowest guard decides where it breaks.
- Strip by name, or strip unconditionally without checking `BuildOptions.Development`.
- Rely on `[SerializeField]` data for a **self-spawned** component; it has none. (A prefab-authored one may use serialized fields freely.)
- Leave an orphan prefab whose only purpose was to carry a now-self-spawning dev component — delete it.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No `#if UNITY_EDITOR || DEVELOPMENT_BUILD` MonoBehaviour reachable from a shipped prefab/scene without a stripper
- [ ] Prefab-hosted dev tools: all components share the same guard, and the root carries the stripper's marker
- [ ] Stripper checks `report == null` (play mode) and `BuildOptions.Development`, and matches by type
- [ ] Self-spawned dev systems are guarded, idempotent, and work from code defaults
- [ ] Prefabs/scenes that existed only to host a now-self-spawning dev component are removed
